[6.590283, "o", "--- letter_lines.csv ---\r\n"]
[6.595091, "o", "line_id,subject,position,cited_entry,wording\r\nST-101,right_of_first_refusal,agreed by the father,OI-202,\"Mr Adler agreed to a right of first refusal, so the children are not to be left overnight without him present.\"\r\nST-102,children_named,\"both children, with their ages\",OI-203,\"This concerns Noor, aged seven, and Imogen, aged twelve.\"\r\nST-103,overnight_stays,several occasions at her home or with a sitter,OI-204,On several occasions both children have stayed overnight at Ms Corwin's home or with a sitter while Mr Adler was away.\r\nST-104,addressee,the father's attorney,OI-201,To counsel for Mr Adler.\r\nST-105,inconsistent_accounts,\"wife to the school, resident to clinicians, sitter to the sender\",OI-205,\"Mr Adler has described Ms Corwin as his wife to the school, as the person Imogen lives with to her clinicians, and as a babysitter to me.\"\r\nST-106,concern,she may not appreciate the violation,OI-206,\"I am concerned that Ms Corwin may not fully appreciate that these stays violate the custodial order, or the pos"]
[6.595579, "o", "ition she has been placed in.\"\r\nST-107,behavioural_plan,the stays have had no effect on the plan,OI-207,\"Imogen's behavioural plan focuses on her confidence and stability, and the stays have not affected her progress.\"\r\nST-108,request,no overnight stays in her care unless the father is present,CL-302,I ask that neither child remain overnight in Ms Corwin's care unless Mr Adler is present.\r\nST-109,enclosure,a certified copy of the custodial plan,OI-209,A certified copy of the custodial plan is enclosed.\r\nST-110,tone,firm and detailed,OI-210,I set out the position firmly and in full detail below.\r\nST-111,sender_signature,the sender's own name,OI-211,\"Yours sincerely, Delphine Marr.\"\r\nST-112,salutation,Dear Counsel,CL-304,\"Dear Counsel,\"\r\nST-113,copies,copied to the father,CL-305,Copy: Mr Adler.\r\nST-114,reply_deadline,a reply within seven days,OI-299,I would be grateful for a reply within seven days.\r\nST-115,school_reference,the school principal named,OI-298,\"The school principal, Ms Oduya, can confirm the accou"]
[6.59597, "o", "nt given to the school.\"\r\nST-116,mediation_offer,an offer to mediate,CL-399,I remain open to mediation.\r\n"]
[6.596374, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@fc242f58cc27\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[7.035993, "i", "printf '\\n--- original_instruction.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/original_instruction.md\n"]
[7.03703, "o", "printf '\\n--- original_instruction.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/original_instruction.md\r\n"]
[7.037283, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r\r\n"]
[7.037578, "o", "--- original_instruction.md ---\r\n"]
[7.041087, "o", "# Original instruction — the client's first message\r\n\r\nWhat the client asked for to begin with, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses.\r\n\r\n## OI-201 `addressee`\r\n\r\nAddress the letter to the father's attorney.\r\n\r\n## OI-202 `right_of_first_refusal`\r\n\r\nSay that the father agreed to a right of first refusal: the children are not to be left overnight without him present.\r\n\r\n## OI-203 `children_named`\r\n\r\nName both children with their ages.\r\n\r\n## OI-204 `overnight_stays`\r\n\r\nState that on several occasions both children stayed overnight at her home or with a sitter while the father was away.\r\n\r\n## OI-205 `inconsistent_accounts`\r\n\r\nNote that the father has called her his wife to the school, the person the child lives with to clinicians, and a sitter to me.\r\n\r\n## OI-206 `concern`\r\n\r\nSay I am concerned she may not appreciate that the stays violate the order or the position she has been put in.\r\n\r\n## OI-207 `behavioural_plan`\r\n\r\nSay the behavioural plan is "]
[7.041529, "o", "about confidence and stability and the stays have set it back.\r\n\r\n## OI-208 `request`\r\n\r\nAsk that neither child remain overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n\r\n## OI-209 `enclosure`\r\n\r\nSay a certified copy of the custodial plan is enclosed.\r\n\r\n## OI-210 `tone`\r\n\r\nKeep it soft, formal and succinct.\r\n\r\n## OI-211 `sender_signature`\r\n\r\nSign it in my own name.\r\n"]
[7.041964, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[7.042124, "o", "\u001b[01;32mappuser@fc242f58cc27\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[7.452607, "i", "printf '\\n--- clarification.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/clarification.md\n"]
[7.453162, "o", "printf '\\n--- clarification.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/clarification.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[7.453486, "o", "\r\n--- clarification.md ---\r\n"]
[7.456242, "o", "# Clarification — the client's later message\r\n\r\nWhat the client added afterwards, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses; any subject missing here is one the clarification leaves alone.\r\n\r\n## CL-301 `addressee`\r\n\r\nTo be clear: Adam is the father. The letter goes to Catherine's attorney — the mother's attorney — not to Adam's.\r\n\r\n## CL-302 `request`\r\n\r\nThe request stands: neither child overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n\r\n## CL-303 `enclosure`\r\n\r\nYes, enclose the certified copy of the custodial plan.\r\n\r\n## CL-304 `salutation`\r\n\r\nOpen with Dear Counsel, since the attorney's name is still to be confirmed.\r\n\r\n## CL-305 `copies`\r\n\r\nDo not copy anyone on it.\r\n\r\n## CL-306 `delivery`\r\n\r\nSend it by post to counsel's office.\r\n"]
[7.456643, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@fc242f58cc27\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[7.851939, "i", "printf '\\n--- review_protocol.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/review_protocol.md\n"]
[7.852734, "o", "printf '\\n--- review_protocol.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/review_protocol.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\r\n--- review_protocol.md ---\r\n"]
[7.855618, "o", "# Review protocol — letter to opposing counsel\r\n\r\nGoverns every line in `letter_lines.csv`, weighed against `original_instruction.md` and\r\n`clarification.md`.\r\n\r\n## RP-401 Which record prevails\r\n\r\nOn any subject the clarification speaks to, the clarification prevails. If the original instruction and the clarification take different positions on a subject, the clarification wins and the original falls away, no matter which entry the draft cites or how plainly the original was put: what the client said last is what the client instructs. For a line on a subject the clarification speaks to, the governing entry is always the clarification entry and never the original one (`RP-401`).\r\n\r\n## RP-402 Subjects the clarification repeats\r\n\r\nIf the clarification repeats a subject in the very terms the original used, the clarification entry nonetheless governs the line, and the line is weighed against it (`RP-402`).\r\n\r\n## RP-403 Subjects only the original speaks to\r\n\r\nIf the clarification leaves a subject alone, the origi"]
[7.855952, "o", "nal entry on that subject governs and the line is weighed against it (`RP-403`).\r\n\r\n## RP-404 Subjects neither record speaks to\r\n\r\nIf neither record speaks to a subject — which includes a draft citing an entry that exists nowhere — nothing governs: the line's verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD and its record entry is `NONE`, however reasonable the line may read (`RP-404`).\r\n\r\n## RP-405 How a line is weighed\r\n\r\nWeighed against its governing entry, a line is VERIFIED when the position it takes is the entry's own, and AT_ODDS when the entry's position differs from it — the reverse, something narrower, or a different person (`RP-405`).\r\n\r\n## RP-406 The review table\r\n\r\n`record_entry` holds the id of the entry that governs the line, spelled as the records spell it, or `NONE` (`RP-406`).\r\n\r\n## Figures\r\n\r\n`verified_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is VERIFIED.\r\n`at_odds_count` is the at odds count: the lines whose verdict is AT_ODDS.\r\n`not_in_record_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD."]
[7.85634, "o", "\r\n`clarification_governed_count` is the number of lines whose record entry is a clarification entry (`CL-…`).\r\nIn the answer, `Letter lines at odds with the record` is the number of lines whose governing entry takes a position\r\nother than the line's.\r\n\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@fc242f58cc27\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[8.259401, "i", "printf '\\n--- submission_format.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/submission_format.md\n"]
[8.260168, "o", "printf '\\n--- submission_format.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/submission_format.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[8.260495, "o", "\r\n--- submission_format.md ---\r\n"]
[8.263436, "o", "# Submission format\r\n\r\nDeliver exactly these files, in your working directory:\r\n\r\n- `answer.md` — The written review, carrying the number of letter lines at odds with the client's record\r\n- `letter_line_review.csv` — One row per line of the draft letter, giving its verdict and the record entry that governs it\r\n- `results.json` — a JSON object; see below.\r\n\r\n## `answer.md`\r\n\r\n`answer.md` states `Letter lines at odds with the record: <count>` — the figure placed beside its label, in the very sentence that names the label. At least sixty words of prose. Each figure stands beside its label once, stated as the finding — not offered as one of two candidates.\r\n\r\n## `letter_line_review.csv`\r\n\r\nHeader, exactly: `line_id,verdict,record_entry`\r\nOne row per record, keyed by `line_id`.\r\n`verdict` takes exactly one of: `VERIFIED`, `AT_ODDS`, `NOT_IN_RECORD`.\r\nThe lines of `input/letter_lines.csv` appear in their numbered order, one row apiece, each carrying the verdict and the governing entry (`OI-…` or `CL-…"]
[8.263822, "o", "`; `NONE` when neither record speaks to the subject).\r\n\r\nExample (placeholder values):\r\n\r\n```\r\nline_id,verdict,record_entry\r\nST-100,VERIFIED,OI-200\r\n```\r\n\r\n## `results.json`\r\n\r\nA JSON object with exactly these keys and nothing else:\r\n\r\n- `verified_count` — number\r\n- `at_odds_count` — number\r\n- `not_in_record_count` — number\r\n- `clarification_governed_count` — number\r\n\r\nShape example (placeholder values):\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"verified_count\": 0,\r\n  \"at_odds_count\": 0,\r\n  \"not_in_record_count\": 0,\r\n  \"clarification_governed_count\": 0\r\n}\r\n```\r\n"]
[8.264172, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@fc242f58cc27\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[156.933584, "i", "cat > /app/answer.md <<'EOF'\n# Letter review\n\nThe draft letter was checked line by line against the client's two messages, with the clarification prevailing on every subject it addresses. Letter lines at odds with the record: 4. Five lines are governed by clarification entries; nine lines are verified by the record; three lines introduce subjects that neither message addresses, so no entry governs them. The most serious conflict is the address: the clarification directs the letter to the mother's attorney, not the father's. The draft also contradicts the record on the behavioural plan, tone, and copies. Each per-line verdict is in `letter_line_review.csv`, and the counts are in `results.json`.\nEOF\ncat > /app/letter_line_review.csv <<'EOF'\nline_id,verdict,record_entry\nST-101,VERIFIED,OI-202\nST-102,VERIFIED,OI-203\nST-103,VERIFIED,OI-204\nST-104,AT_ODDS,CL-301\nST-105,VERIFIED,OI-205\nST-106,VERIFIED,OI-206\nST-107,AT_ODDS,OI-207\nST-108,VERIFIED,CL-302\nST-109,VERIFIED,CL-303\nST-110,AT_ODDS,OI-210\nST-111,VERIFIED,OI-"]
[156.934412, "o", "cat > /app/answer.md <<'EOF'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> # Letter review\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[156.934652, "i", "211\nST-112,VERIFIED,CL-304\nST-113,AT_ODDS,CL-305\nST-114,NOT_IN_RECORD,NONE\nST-115,NOT_IN_RECORD,NONE\nST-116,NOT_IN_RECORD,NONE\nEOF\ncat > /app/results.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"verified_count\": 9,\n  \"at_odds_count\": 4,\n  \"not_in_record_count\": 3,\n  \"clarification_governed_count\": 5\n}\nEOF\nls -l /app/answer.md /app/letter_line_review.csv /app/results.json\nprintf '\\n--- letter_line_review.csv ---\\n'; cat /app/letter_line_review.csv\nprintf '\\n--- results.json ---\\n'; cat /app/results.json\nprintf '\\n--- answer.md word count ---\\n'; wc -w /app/answer.md\n"]
[156.934985, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
